at the heart of a snowblower are  rotating blades called augers the left  and right augur spiral in opposite  directions in order to move snow toward  the middle there a fan called an  impeller swallows the snow and blows it  out a chute each author starts out as a  partially formed steel disc just under  an eighth of an inch thick  a press applies about 200 tons of  pressure to form the augers final shape  meanwhile robots are welding together  the steel housing that will eventually  case the augers it takes the robots just  55 seconds to weld a housing  next the housings undergo an  electrostatic paint process powder paint  laced with a positive electrical charge  is drawn to the housings which are  negatively charged then the housing  spend 15 minutes in an oven which bakes  on the paint in another part of the  factory workers are assembling the snow  blowers gas-powered engine and  transmission  after bolting on the impeller they  attached a large steel axle to the  transmission driven sprocket then they  slide the now painted auger and hub  units onto the axle  now it's time to bolt the auger assembly  into the snow blower housing the  impeller slides into a cup that's  centered at the back of the housing then  they screw the housing to the  transmission frame the last component is  the snowblowers handle there's a lever  for selecting different speeds and  clutch levers that can be locked down to  direct the wheels and augers there's  also a headlight so that you'll be  visible to oncoming cars while clearing  snow at night there's also a lever that  lets you choose the direction to blow  the snow
 

